Q: Create file but if name exists add number A: <p>Here is an answer that does not contain a race condition from checking if the file exists in advance. It caps out at 999 attempts so the program won't hang forever if the file fails to create, the same as a shell usually would.</p>
<pre><code>import os


def create_file_unique(path, read=False, binary=False, min_=1, max_=1000):
  mode = ''.join(('x', '+' if read else '', 'b' if binary else ''))

  def increment_file_name():
    root, ext = os.path.splitext(path)

    return (path if num == min_ else '%s (%d)%s' % (root, num, ext
      ) for num in range(min_, max_))

  for i in increment_file_name():
    try:
      return open(i, mode)
    except FileExistsError:
      pass

  raise IOError('The file %r could not be created uniquely.' % path)


if __name__ == '__main__':
  with create_file_unique('unique.txt') as f:
    print('Hello World', file=f)
</code></pre>
<p>By default, it will open the file with write-only permissions in text mode. Specify <code>read</code> as True for read and write permissions, and <code>binary</code> as True for binary mode. (Note that having read-only permissions would be useless - after all, you just created the file blank!)</p>
<p>Be aware that for the sake of simplicity, this answer (as well as the other answers here) assumes the filename passed in is &quot;bare.&quot; That is to say, it will not handle the scenario where the filename passed in already contains a number in parenthesis, like <code>create_file_unique('unique (2).txt')</code>. In that case, this function will add another number on the end, resulting in <code>unique (2) (2).txt</code>. This differs from the conventional behaviour of OS functions intended to accomplish this task, like <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/shlobj_core/nf-shlobj_core-pathyetanothermakeuniquename" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PathYetAnotherMakeUniqueName</a> from the Windows shell, which will check if the filename passed in already contains a number in parenthesis and will update that number in place if it finds one.</p>
<p>If you're willing to forego sequential filenames, you could use <code>NamedTemporaryFile</code> with a prefix, suffix, and deletion turned off, in order to accomplish something similar.</p>
<pre><code>import os
from tempfile import NamedTemporaryFile


def create_file_unique(path, mode='w+b'):
  root, ext = os.path.splitext(path)

  return NamedTemporaryFile(
    delete=False, mode=mode,
    prefix=root, suffix=ext, dir=''
  )
</code></pre>

Q: How can I set a variable primary color dynamically while using Tailwind colors in different shades? A: <p>Sure! You could do something like:</p>
<h3>Least Involved</h3>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@utility bg-primary-* {
  /* For bg-primary-200, --shade would be 200 */
  --shade: --value(int);
  background-color: /* do some calculation with `--shade` and `--primary-color` here */
}
</code></pre>
<p>Of course, this means you'd need to define the above dynamic utilities for all the color utility class names. Further, you'd need to figure out how you want the shades to be calculated.</p>
<h3>Pre-process</h3>
<p>If you want to avoid manually doing this, you could have some step <em>before</em> Tailwind that converts:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@my-theme {
  --primary-color: blue;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Into:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@theme {
  --color-primary-50: /* some shade here */;
  --color-primary-100: /* some shade here */;
  --color-primary-200: /* some shade here */;
  …
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then pass the resulting CSS as the input into Tailwind CSS.</p>
<p>Using some arbitrary at-rule name like <code>@my-theme</code> means this is easier to track in your converter code, and ensures no collision with other tooling.</p>
<h3>JS plugin</h3>
<p>You could also use a <a href="https://v3.tailwindcss.com/docs/plugins#exposing-options" rel="nofollow noreferrer">legacy JS plugin</a> to define shades from some base color:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@plugin &quot;./my-plugin&quot; {
  primary: 'blue',
}

/**
 * Placeholder variables to register the tokens into Tailwind.
 * 
 * The actual values would be defined by the JS plugin.
 */
@theme {
  --color-primary-50: black;
  --color-primary-100: black;
  --color-primary-200: black;
  …
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import plugin from 'tailwindcss/plugin';

export default plugin.withOptions(({ primary })) =&gt; ({ addBase }) =&gt; {
  addBase({
    ':root': {
      '--color-primary-50': /* Calculate shade here */,
      '--color-primary-100': /* Calculate shade here */,
      '--color-primary-200': /* Calculate shade here */,
       …
    },
  });
);
</code></pre>

Q: How to correctly push jobs to BullMQ from Laravel? A: <p>The wait list should contain the job id:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$jobId = Str::uuid()-&gt;toString();
Redis::rpush('bull:compass-queue:wait', $jobId);
</code></pre>
<p>You also need to push the job fields to the <code>bull:compass-queue:jobId</code> hash:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$jobKey = &quot;bull:compass-queue:{$jobId}&quot;;
$jobFields = [
    'name' =&gt; 'connect-account',
    'data' =&gt; json_encode([ /** Job payload data */]),
    'opts' =&gt; json_encode([ /** the job options */]),
    'timestamp' =&gt; $this-&gt;timestamp,
    'attemptsMade' =&gt; 0,
    //...
];
Redis::hMSet($jobKey, $jobFields);
</code></pre>
<p>I recommend adding the <a href="https://github.com/taskforcesh/bullmq/tree/master/php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Bullmq PHP library to your project</a>. Then you can write submit your job:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>use BullMQ\Queue;

try {
    $queue = new Queue('my-queue');
    $job = $queue-&gt;add('connect-account', $data);
} catch (\RuntimeException $e) {
    echo &quot;Error: &quot; . $e-&gt;getMessage() . &quot;\n&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
